WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.865
A quadratic loop for Longest Increasing Subsequence is too slow when N reaches two hundred thousand.

00:00:06.865 --> 00:00:16.407
Instead, maintain an array tails where tails[k] holds the smallest possible ending value of an increasing subsequence of length k plus one.

00:00:16.407 --> 00:00:20.800
Because tails stays sorted, we can update it with binary search.

00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:28.573
For each element x, use bisect_left on tails to find the first ending value greater than or equal to x.

00:00:28.573 --> 00:00:36.518
Replace that entry to keep future extensions as easy as possible, or append x if it extends the longest chain.

00:00:36.518 --> 00:00:41.573
Use bisect_right instead when non-decreasing subsequences are allowed.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:48.202
Longest Common Subsequence compares prefixes of two strings A and B on a two-dimensional grid.

00:00:48.202 --> 00:00:56.581
When the current characters match, extend the diagonal state by one; otherwise take the maximum of skipping either character.

00:00:56.581 --> 00:01:01.140
Keep only two rows in memory when only the final length is required.

00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:08.383
Edit distance generalizes LCS by assigning explicit costs to insertion, deletion, and substitution.

00:01:08.383 --> 00:01:15.928
Transition from the top, left, and diagonal neighbors by taking the minimum cost among all three operations.

00:01:15.928 --> 00:01:21.238
This same alignment table powers diff tools and approximate string matching.
